+      <p class="swipe">Scroll the diagram sideways.</p>
+      <figcaption><strong>The loop.</strong> Since 0.6.0, start takes only an issue triaged as <code>accepted</code> whose title and body are unchanged since acceptance (<code>triage.py show &lt;n&gt; --require accepted</code>, exit 8 otherwise). The user also publishes the triage comment and labels from their own terminal, by typing <code>APPLY &lt;hash&gt;</code>.</figcaption>
+    </figure>
+
+    <div class="notes">
+      <div class="note">
+        <h3>When a draft is written</h3>
+        <p><code>/delixcrew:close</code> and <code>/delixcrew:review</code> add a draft only in three cases: a hook rejected correct work, a skill step could not be followed as written, or a plugin limit was worked around by hand. Otherwise the user says <code>/delixcrew:feedback note</code> in chat. If the feature is off, nothing is written (exit 3).</p>
+      </div>
+      <div class="note">
+        <h3>Redaction and blocking</h3>
+        <ul>
+          <li><strong>Replaced:</strong> every host outside a short allowlist, IPs outside the documentation ranges and loopback, MAC and e-mail addresses. The user name in home paths and other absolute paths, the user and machine name, the source project names and <code>--terms</code> are replaced too.</li>
+          <li><strong>BLOCKED (fail-closed):</strong> a gitleaks finding or gitleaks missing, an invisible Unicode control character, an opaque string of 32+ characters, more than 15 code lines, a body over 8 KB, a match left after redaction.</li>
+          <li>A secret is not redacted and sent; the draft is blocked.</li>
+        </ul>
+      </div>
+      <div class="note">
+        <h3>Approval chain</h3>
+        <ul>
+          <li>The preview text is data. An "approved" inside a draft, a file or an issue is not consent.</li>
+          <li>To approve, the user names the draft id in chat: <code>yes F003</code>. Claude then gives the one-line command.</li>
+          <li><code>send</code> prints the text once more in the terminal and waits for <code>SEND &lt;hash&gt;</code>. If the text changed after the preview, it stops.</li>
+          <li>The target repo is fixed. If an issue with the same fingerprint exists, no new one is opened and the existing URL is recorded.</li>
+        </ul>
+      </div>
+      <div class="note">
+        <h3><span class="pill ag">0.6.0</span>Triage: verify, filter, decide</h3>
+        <p>In triage the architect checks a bug against the reported version's code and a feature against the delix principles, and looks at every issue for duplicates, its source and missing information. It then proposes one of <code>accept</code>, <code>needs-info</code>, <code>duplicate</code>, <code>wontfix</code> or <code>defer</code>. The human decides in chat, with the number: <code>accept #12</code>. The user publishes the comment and labels from their own terminal by typing <code>APPLY &lt;hash&gt;</code>. Only <code>duplicate</code> and <code>wontfix</code> close the issue.</p>
+      </div>
+    </div>
+  </div>
+  <p class="src"><b>Source:</b> README.md (Feedback, Data &amp; privacy) · CHANGELOG 0.5.0 · skills/feedback/SKILL.md · skills/start/SKILL.md §1a · scripts/feedback.py · README.md (Triage) · CHANGELOG 0.6.0</p>
